.TH SYS_INFO 2
.SH NAME
sys_info \- system information (SYS_INFO)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_info(mk_info_t *in);

typedef struct {
    uint64_t uptime_ns;               /* time since boot */
    uint64_t pages_total, pages_free; /* memory, 4 KiB pages */
    int32_t self_pid, self_uid;
    int32_t nprocs, nthreads, ncpus;
    uint32_t hwcap;                   /* HWCAP_* */
    int32_t ecores;                   /* E-cores (0: all alike) */
    int32_t cpu, cpu_e;               /* the caller's CPU now */
    char root[40];                    /* boot option root=GUID */
} mk_info_t;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_info
fills
.I in
with the state of the system: the time since boot, memory in 4 KiB
pages, the caller's pid and uid, the numbers of processes, threads and
processors, how many of the processors are E-cores (0 where all cores are
alike), the processor the caller runs on now and whether it is an E-core,
and the boot option
.B root=
(the partition SIEOS runs from, or "").
.PP
.I hwcap
tells what the processor can do, as the kernel read it at boot (some
AArch64 ID registers cannot be read from user mode):
.B HWCAP_AVX2
(and the kernel saves AVX state),
.B HWCAP_FMA
and
.B HWCAP_AVXVNNI
on x86-64;
.B HWCAP_DOTPROD
(SDOT/UDOT: the Pi 5 has it, the Pi 4 not) and
.B HWCAP_LSE
on AArch64.
.PP
Anyone may call it;
.BR mem (1)
and
.BR uptime (1)
print it.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I in
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_tasks (2),
.BR sys_clock (2),
.BR mem (1),
.BR uptime (1)
